  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At the bar counter, Vika completely forgets that banquet clients came by yesterday, and Nastya gently reveals that she and Kostya already figured out Vika's pregnancy secret. Listen to the Russian conversation.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Тимуру надо отдать деньги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Короче, он знает за что.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Виктория Сергеевна, к вам приходили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "По поводу какого банкета?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Они вчера приходили и заказывали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Они хотели внести изменения в меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они были здесь вчера?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Вы не волнуйтесь, я приняла заказ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Вам сейчас нельзя нервничать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Откуда ты знаешь? Уже видно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Нет, пока ещё не очень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мы с Костей начали подозревать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сделай, пожалуйста, кофе для столиков.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Тимуру надо отдать деньги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to give the money to Timur.' In Russian, the idea of 'proper name' flows naturally into 'money' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Короче, он знает за что.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In short, he knows what it is for.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'long story short' and finishing on 'what'. Nastya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Виктория Сергеевна, к вам приходили.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Victoria Sergeevna, people came to see you.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'proper name' and 'you' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "По поводу какого банкета?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Regarding which banquet?' The grammar packs 'according to' and 'of the banquet' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Они вчера приходили и заказывали.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They came yesterday and placed an order.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'they' and 'yesterday' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Они хотели внести изменения в меню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They wanted to make changes to the menu.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'they' before landing on 'menu'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Они были здесь вчера?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Were they here yesterday?' Vika brings out the contrast between 'they' and 'to be' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Вы не волнуйтесь, я приняла заказ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry, I took the order.' Nastya chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'order' to keep a polite distance.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Вам сейчас нельзя нервничать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You must not get nervous right now.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'you' and 'now' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Откуда ты знаешь? Уже видно?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How do you know? Does it show already?' Vika raises the pitch on 'one can see' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Нет, пока ещё не очень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, not very much yet.' Notice how Russian expresses 'no' and 'very' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мы с Костей начали подозревать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostya and I started to suspect.' Nastya anchors the line on 'we', while 'to suspect' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Сделай, пожалуйста, кофе для столиков.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Please make coffee for the tables.' Vika brings out the contrast between 'make' and 'please' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
